const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "cv:1": [
    "In Mindelo, on the island of São Vicente, people sometimes talk about the witch. But the version told to children is gentle. It fits in the street, not in a scare. It is not a woman on a broomstick flying over the harbour. It is the tongue of people when envy dresses up as a look. They call it the evil eye. Or a djunta-mon that is broken apart, not with shouting, but with a simple gesture of protection. Mindelo is a city of morna and of sailors. It has nights when talk on the quay seems bigger than it is. Urban fear is born there, in words.",
    "The girl Nanda had a new blue dress, for the port festival. The neighbour Rute looked at her and spoke loudly, for half the street to hear. ‘Anyone who dresses like that wants to seem more than they are.’ Nanda tripped on a step. The dress got dusty, and the festival lost its colour that day. Rute went home happy for half a minute. Then she had a weight in her chest, with no name for it. In the street, some people were already saying the witch had passed by. Rute's grandmother did not go looking for film candles. She went to fetch her granddaughter from the kitchen.",
    "The grandmother explained slowly. In Cape Verde, the Crioulo language mixes Catholic faith with old ways of protecting the home. Not every bad mood comes from a stranger. Often the witch is envy with a name. Rute's sentence had hurt. This city of sailors believes what is said at night, because words travel like boats. There was no need to hate Nanda, or to fear a shadow on the pier. There was a need to undo what the mouth had done. The djunta-mon, the bad luck gathered together, is broken with truth, not with more poison.",
    "Rute took bread to Nanda's house and said, with no audience: ‘I was envious. I'm sorry. The dress suited you.’ Nanda accepted the bread. The grandmother put water on both their foreheads and prayed softly. It was a gentle rite that frightened nobody. The dust came out of the dress in the wash. The next festival went better. No spell was undone in the air. The bad sentence was simply no longer in the middle of things. Mindelo went on with its port nights. Only that street became a little lighter.",
    "With this tale, the Cape Verdean child learns not to be envious out loud. In the gentle version, the witch of Mindelo warns the heart before she warns the dark street. If you feel the urge to make someone with a lovely thing seem smaller, stop the sentence. The evil eye is often just this: a look that did not know how to keep quiet. Share the bread, tell the truth, and let the protection of the home be simple. The morna can sing of longing. It does not need to sing of envy. The city is better when the tongue does not dress up as a witch.",
  ],
  "cv:2": [
    "Off the coast of Praia, on the island of Santiago, the oldest people speak of a ship of lights. It is seen on certain nights and never reaches the harbour. It is not a picture-postcard pirate ship, with a flag and laughter. It is a boat that memory has put at sea. For centuries, Cape Verde was a crossroads of Atlantic routes. Sailors, storms and many people who had not chosen their journey all passed this way. The dead of the sea, says the gentle legend, are still looking for a coast. That is why the lights appear and never dock.",
    "The version told to children shows no horrors. It shows a distant boat, made of bright dots. It reminds us that the ocean charged a fare to those who should not have had to pay it. Praia looks at the harbour and the lighthouse, and the legend asks us to tell one light from the other. The lighthouse wants you to arrive. The ghost ship does not want company. It wants to be remembered. If you follow it in a small canoe, the story says, the coast moves away. The night is then left without a road. This is not a chasing game. It is a warning to be respectful.",
    "One night the boy Djo saw the lights from his window. He wanted to go down to the beach and point at them, as if they were fireworks. His grandfather put a hand on his shoulder. ‘Not every light at sea wants to be followed,’ he said. They sat on the wall. The grandfather explained, in simple words, that many people had crossed that ocean by force. Storms had also taken working boats. The lights were remembrance, not an invitation. Djo felt sad in the right way, without fear of a monster. Sad like someone who understands the sea has old debts.",
    "Instead of going after the glow, they lit a small oil lamp at home. It was turned towards the harbour, like someone saying: we saw, we remember, we stay on land. The lights out at sea went out before dawn, as always. No ship came ashore. The grandfather did not swear the ghost was made of wood and sail. He swore the story was there so that old suffering would not become a game. The next day, Djo looked at the real lighthouse and learned its name. One light can save. Another only asks for silence.",
    "In Praia, if someone points at a glow that does not come closer, do not run for a boat. Listen. Cape Verde keeps this legend because the island group knows crossing, loss and longing. The ghost ship is neither hero nor film villain. It is the memory of forced crossings and of storms, set in lights. It must not be lost in the dark. The child sees the lighthouse and learns the difference. Not every light at sea wants to be followed. Some only want you not to pretend the ocean has forgotten.",
  ],
  "cv:3": [
    "The morna was not born in a time of gods and giants. It is living folklore, from roughly the nineteenth century onwards. We say so plainly, so as not to dress it up as an ancient myth. Even so, Cape Verde tells of its birth as if telling a family legend. Some point to the island of Boa Vista. Others point to São Vicente, the harbour of Mindelo. Every version has a lament of love and of departure, a voice that does not shout. Every version has a boat that should have come back and did not. From this waiting a way of singing was born.",
    "Imagine a woman on the quay, watching a horizon that looks the same every day. The name of the one who left no longer fits into an ordinary conversation. The conversation ends, but the absence does not. So she sings. The note is long and Crioulo, full of longing, and the neighbours recognise their own lives inside it. Someone arrives with a cavaquinho, a small four-string guitar. Someone arrives with a violin. Nobody marks the day on a calendar. The morna stays, because departure, on the islands, was not a single case. It was the bread of many homes. The song took hold of what speech could not hold.",
    "The people made the morna their own fado, without asking anyone's permission. It is not a copy. It is the Cape Verdean lament, with the sea in the middle of its phrases. The nine islands listen to one another. Later, Cesária Évora took that voice to the world, barefoot and firm. Many people abroad learned the name of the morna from her mouth. Children in Cape Verde hear Cesária the way they hear a grandmother. She is not a poster, she is a person in the big family of music. Here the modern legend and the true history hold hands without getting in each other's way.",
    "If you ask exactly where it was born, Boa Vista and São Vicente can smile and not argue. In this tale, the birth is not a single backyard. The whole island group needed a note that would call back those who had gone. The woman on the quay may have had a thousand names. The cavaquinho may have started at a party or at a mourning. What matters is the core, and the morna is longing that does not give up. It does not push the boats. It stays in the voice, waiting. It teaches the listener not to be ashamed to say that someone is missing.",
    "Cape Verde explains itself in this song better than in speeches. Nine islands, one sea, one long note that still calls the names of those who left. If you hear a morna, do not ask only for the fast dance. Sit down. Let the phrase finish. The story of the morna is the story of departure. It is also the love that stays on the quay with its feet on the stone. It is not an ancient myth, and it does not need to be. It is living folklore. As long as a voice is on the quay, someone still names the boat that did not return.",
  ],
  "cv:4": [
    "In the deep sea off São Vicente, the fishermen of Mindelo tell of a shark. It is not the villain of a cartoon. It is a water neighbour. First, someone leaves a small offering. It may be the first fish or a serious greeting before setting out. Then the shark pushes the shoal towards the net. On foggy days, it follows the boat to the point. Respect for the shark is an old law of the quay. Beside the tale lives a fair fear, because a big animal in the deep sea is not a puppet. The legend chooses an ally, without blunting the tooth.",
    "Before sunrise, the boy Lito went out with his uncle in a small boat. The fog swallowed the island. Lito, nervous, wanted to shout and slap the water with the oar. His uncle held his wrist. ‘The agreement first,’ he said. They put the first fish from the net back into the water, whole and without anger. A dark back passed beside the boat, long and calm. It did not turn its mouth towards them. It stayed a little ahead, cutting through the fog. The uncle followed that shadow until the point of the harbour appeared. The shark sank without a show. The boat arrived.",
    "On the quay, Lito told the story shouting, as if he had tamed a monster. His uncle corrected him in front of the others, without shaming him too much. ‘He is not yours. You did not tame him. He followed because the sea was in order.’ The children learned both halves. The creature is not a cartoon monster, and not a pet to be touched. It is a neighbour. In the tale it can help anyone who treats the water as a home. In real life it can frighten anyone who provokes it or mistakes it for a toy. Fair fear and the offering both fit in the same boat.",
    "There is also the final warning. Some people treat the sea like rubbish. They throw dirty scraps and laugh at the depths. They should expect no guide at all. The legend says that on that day the friend turns into nothing but tooth. It is not because it is bad by nature, but because the agreement has been broken. Lito began to keep the rubbish in the boat until they reached land. He left the first fish when the net came up full. Not to buy a miracle. To remember that the sea is the biggest street, with residents who do not speak the language of the square.",
    "Cape Verde, a land of fishermen, needs this alliance told with good sense. If you go to the harbour of Mindelo, look at the deep water. Do not throw anything in, and do not put your hand in for no reason. The friendly shark is a tale of respect, not an invitation to swim beside it. The water neighbour likes distance and a clean sea. To anyone who keeps that side of the bargain, the legend lets them reach the point. Those who do not are left with nothing but fear. Both lessons are the quay's gift: courage yes, games with the tooth never.",
  ],
  "gw:0": [
    "In the stories of Guinea-Bissau, N'Pará walks with the animals of the forest. He walks with them the way one walks with neighbours. He has no king's cloak and no hurry to give orders. One day the tabanca, the village, needed rules for a new field. The field was beside the bolanha, the rice field that feeds everyone. N'Pará called the leopard, the goat and the hen. Each arrived certain that the law should be made to fit them. The leopard spoke softly and showed his tooth without using it. The goat scraped the ground. The hen was already counting grains as if the sack were hers alone.",
    "The leopard said: ‘The law belongs to the strongest. The small ones thank me.’ The goat said: ‘The law belongs to whoever works the bush. The leopard sleeps by day. The field is mine, and the hen gets a corner.’ Then the hen spoke. ‘The law belongs to whoever finds the grain. I dig and eat.’ The argument looked like adults arguing when nobody wants to lose their turn. The bolanha nearby was waiting for water and for agreement. Without agreement, the rice does not know who it is growing for.",
    "N'Pará heard them out, then stood up from his tree trunk. The law of the forest is not the law of the biggest tooth. Nor is it the law of whoever shouts that he worked most. It is not the law of the one who reached the grain first. It is the law that lets everyone eat. He marked three equal parts in the field. Each had one task, and all had the same day of rest. The leopard would stand guard for one night only. The goat would open the path through the bush. The hen would mind the grains without hiding them. Nobody was the only owner.",
    "The leopard grumbled, but waited his turn. At least he did in this tale, which is the place where waiting is taught. The goat stopped pushing the hen. The hen stopped treating the sack as a secret. When the rain came, the bolanha filled and the new field gave its harvest. In another village, each one pulled the law towards himself. There the rice field dried up and the forest closed its short cut. The children of Guinea-Bissau hear the animals arguing and recognise the home and the market. The moral grows in the rice, slowly, like the grain itself.",
    "If you are in the middle of sharing something, do not make the rule fit only you. Remember N'Pará. The small one who talks with the animals does not win with his nails. He wins because he puts the bowl where everyone can reach it. Without agreement, the bolanha dries up. With agreement, even the leopard waits. Guinea-Bissau tells this in the tabancas because the life of the rice needs many hands. The forest is not a kingdom with only one tooth. It is a neighbourhood. N'Pará walks on once the circle is fair. The three then learn, unwillingly, that eating together lasts longer than eating first.",
  ],
  "gw:1": [
    "In the Geba river, wide and full of mangroves, there lived a small turtle that everyone called wise. The hippo boasted of its strength in fresh water. The river shark boasted of its teeth in the current. Both laughed at her because she was slow and her shell frightened no one. The turtle did not argue by shouting. ‘I bet I can pull you with a rope until you drag me along,’ she told each one separately. The hippo agreed, so as to laugh later. The shark agreed, so as to laugh later. Neither knew about the other. That was the old trick, cousin to Ijapa's.",
    "In the morning, the turtle gave one end of the rope to the hippo by the calm pool. ‘When I pull, you start,’ she said. Then she went through the mangroves without hurrying. She gave the other end to the shark, further down, where the water already tastes of salt. In the middle, she tied the rope to a strong root. She sat in the shade, like a judge, not an athlete. When she tugged a little on both ends, each giant thought the turtle was challenging him. The hippo pulled. The shark pulled. The rope went as tight as an argument.",
    "They pulled for a long time. The water boiled in circles, the branches shook, and neither of them moved forward. The strength of one ate the strength of the other. The turtle did not even get her feet muddy. When the giants were tired and angry, she untied the knot on the root. She appeared on the bank, spotless. ‘Enough,’ she said. ‘You were pulling each other. I just marked the rope.’ The hippo saw the shark. The shark saw the hippo. There was no fight and no wound. There was comic shame and a great silence on the Geba, which carries the lesson down to the sea.",
    "The Bijagó, Balanta and Fula peoples each have their clever animals. The turtle crosses all these homes, because slow cleverness is easy to see in any language. Guinea-Bissau is water and mangroves. Anyone who reads the tides does not need to be the biggest. The child of the Geba laughs at the tired giants, then understands the message. The small judge in a shell does not need a concrete courtroom. She needs a rope and patience. She also needs the sense not to enter a test of strength designed for her to lose.",
    "At some point you may be small in front of two big ones who boast. Do not try to pull both of them at once with your arms. Do as the turtle did, and let their boasting meet in the middle. The Geba is still wide, and the tide rises and falls without asking the strong for permission. The turtle goes back to the mud, slowly, now without an audience. She did not become queen. She kept the right on her side. In Guinea-Bissau, that difference counts. The small one who thinks saves their body and teaches the big ones without taking their home away.",
  ],
  "gw:2": [
    "In Guinea-Bissau, the cashew paints the fields yellow and red, and the smell of the season fills the tabancas. The fruit was not always sweet. The legend says that at first it was bitter, so bitter that children pulled faces and guests refused the bowl. The tree saw this and did not get loudly angry. It waited for a person who understood the law of the house. The nut, stuck to the fruit, was not eaten raw either. It asked for fire. There were two problems, and both had to do with the way people welcome others, not just with hunger.",
    "One year an old guest arrived at the tabanca, last in the queue. The pots were already nearly empty. The adults gave him what rice was left and passed the cashew along. They laughed at the face he was going to pull. The girl Sali did not laugh. She took her own bowl, the least bitter she could find. She put it in the guest's hands before serving her cousins. She said only: ‘You eat first.’ The old man tasted it. He did not pull a face. He looked at the tree. That night, the spirit of the cashew moved through the field without a sound.",
    "In the morning, the fruit was sweet. Those who tasted it stayed silent with astonishment. Sali understood that the tree had been testing the people, not the other way round. The spirit sweetened the cashew because someone had offered it to the last in the queue. But the second gift remained, and that is fire. The raw nut is not eaten. The fire of the village looks after it, with someone who knows how. Only then does it go into the food. The legend is not a recipe to play with in the backyard. It is a warning: the fruit and the nut are not the same race.",
    "The truth of the plant and the rite of hospitality meet in this tale. The cashew came to grow rich in this land. The story gave it a law so children would not treat it as a sweet with no past. Without a guest, the fruit would turn bitter again. With a guest served last and yet first in Sali's bowl, the field stayed generous. In season, Guinea-Bissau smells of this household miracle. Families dry, share and respect the fire the nut asks for. The hurry to eat everything raw has no place in the legend.",
    "If someone offers you cashew, remember Sali and the last in the queue. The sweetness had a price of kindness, and it involved sharing. In this story, the tree tests people before it sweetens the ground. And if you see the nut, leave it to those who know how to use the fire of the house. It is not a game. It is the second gift, as important as the first. Guinea-Bissau keeps both lessons on the same branch. Welcoming a guest changes the taste, and not everything a tree gives goes straight to your mouth. Some things wait for their hour.",
  ],
  "gw:3": [
    "In Guinea-Bissau, the palm tree gives the bunch, the red dendém oil and palm wine. That is why it feeds the whole tabanca. The people speak of a spirit, the irã, that lives in it and sees who climbs. The irã does not appear with a film face. It is noticed in the wine that turns sour. It is noticed in the bunch that the greedy one cannot keep. It is noticed in the foot that slips, when the rope was put there to shut others out. The real palm tree is already dangerous work. The legend gives that care a meaning.",
    "A man called Iero wanted the big palm tree only for himself. He tied a cloth round the trunk. ‘Whoever climbs after me is stealing,’ he told the tabanca. At dawn he climbed with the rope round his waist, singing about profit. Up there, the wine pot came out cloudy and sour. The bunch he pulled seemed heavier than it was. Nobody pushed him. The irã, invisible, only took away the certainty from his feet. Iero slipped and fell onto the pile of dry leaves. He got up whole, dirty and ashamed. It was a fall that taught a lesson.",
    "The old people did not laugh out loud. ‘In the tale, the irã throws down anyone who blocks the tree of everyone. In life, the fall comes without any spirit, if you climb greedily.’ Iero drank the sour wine in front of the circle. Then he took the cloth off the trunk. From that day, the morning wine went back to whoever had their turn. The oil went into the common pot on feast days. The palm tree did not become shorter. It became the village's again. The spirit, satisfied or simply just, stopped souring the pot.",
    "The child sees father or mother climb with the right rope, slowly. The child hears the name of the irã before the first step. It is to remember that the dendém, the wine and the bunch are goods of the big house. They are not for a single lunch. Without the spirit, the climb already asks for respect for the body. With it, the danger gains sense: you do not climb to hide things. If the rope belongs to the tabanca, the way down also brings sharing. Iero began to wait for his morning, like the others. His song about profit stayed on the ground.",
    "The legend is a law of the fields. The palm tree gives a lot, and so it tempts people to lock things away. The irã opens. If you have something the whole house needs, do not put your name on it. Do not act as if the root obeyed you. Climb carefully, come down with the fair share, and leave the rest in the common pot. Guinea-Bissau smells of this agreement when the oil sizzles in the pan. The spirit of the palm tree does not ask for terror. It asks that abundance not be locked away. Those who lock it go sour. Those who share climb safely.",
  ],
  "gw:4": [
    "In the mangroves of Guinea-Bissau, the fox and the crocodile argued about who was king. The fox said: ‘The land is mine. I run through the grass and I choose the path. You are just a trunk that drags along.’ The crocodile said: ‘The water is mine. You are just a visitor in a hurry.’ Neither wanted the shore. There, mud is not quite ground and not quite wave. Each spoke of his own centre, where he felt big. Meanwhile the tide rose and fell without choosing any king.",
    "The fox had an idea he thought was clever. He invited the crocodile to the dry grass, in the high sun. ‘If you are a real king, rule here too.’ The crocodile went. On dry land, his step lost its swagger, his body grew heavy, and the fox danced around him laughing. The crocodile did not attack. He went back, angry and slow. The next day, the invitation was reversed: ‘Come to my river, if you are so fine.’ The fox went into the water. The current took away her grace and her paws gave no orders. She came out sneezing, with no crown at all.",
    "The two of them sat on the shore, wet in different ways, and felt the shame at the same moment. Away from their own ground, each lost their swagger. The mangrove was not a kingdom cut in two. It was a treaty. It was land that is water when the tide rises, and water that is land when the tide falls. The fox said: ‘I keep the grass, you keep the depths. The shore belongs to whoever arrives at the right time, with no owner.’ The crocodile agreed. There was no fight and no tale-biting. There was an agreement, which is harder and more useful.",
    "The children of the Bijagós islands see the crocodile and the land mixing in the same steps. They understand the legend without a map. Anyone who wants to be king of both places loses both. They cannot be dry and deep at the same time. Anyone who shares the tide gets supper. The fox hunts in her time. The crocodile rests in his. On the shore, the two pass by without turning the meeting into a war over titles. The mangrove of Guinea-Bissau is exactly this: a double house, where the stubbornness of a single name has no room.",
    "If you want to rule over everything, on land and in water, remember these two. An invitation to someone else's ground does not prove greatness. It proves the limit. The shore, at the right hour, is enough for both and still has room for the birds. Guinea-Bissau tells of the fox and the crocodile to teach the shore, not to pick a winner. King of one place is already a lot. King of two, without agreement, is left with no supper and no swagger. Share the tide. The mangrove, generous, lets stay those who do not steal its name.",
  ],
  "st:0": [
    "On the island of São Tomé, the forest closes so tightly that the sky barely gets in. In the middle of the woods rises Pico Cão Grande, a needle of rock that is almost vertical. Some call it the devil's old finger. Others call it a stone dog standing still and howling. Others call it a giant that time turned to stone. Hunters, lost among the trunks, use the peak as a green lighthouse. When the tip appears between the leaves, you already know which way home is.",
    "On the plantation, the boy Mito saw that stone tooth above the trees every morning. His grandfather said the peak was an animal standing still, not just a hill to own. ‘You don't tame it,’ he explained. ‘You greet it from far away.’ Mito wanted to climb it just to boast to his cousins. The grandfather shook his head. For climbers, the climb is a real test. For the island people, looking is already enough. Looking is enough, with respect.",
    "One day the mist swallowed the paths and two hunters could not find the plantation. Mito climbed a high root, saw the dark needle and pointed. ‘The dog is over there,’ he said. ‘The house is on the sea side.’ They followed that green lighthouse and came out before night. Nobody had beaten the peak. It was the peak that had guided the island. It has done so since the forest grew closed and every path started to look the same.",
    "Later, people came from outside with ropes, to measure the needle and reach the top. Mito stayed below with his grandfather, watching the clouds cover the tip. The old man said the stone lets itself be seen. But it does not like to be treated as a trophy. When the wind climbs the face of the peak, it really does sound like a short howl. Mito did not laugh. He greeted the stone dog, as if greeting a neighbour who cannot be kept on a lead.",
    "São Tomé and Príncipe tells of Cão Grande as an animal that stopped in the middle of the island. The child of the plantation sees the tooth above the woods and learns the greeting, not the conquest. There are things on this ground that are not tamed. They are only greeted, with head up and quiet voice. The lighthouse is still green between the leaves. Anyone who gets lost looks for the needle. Anyone who finds it gives thanks and walks on, without putting a collar on it.",
  ],
  "st:1": [
    "The seven-coloured parrot is the rare bird of São Tomé, and in the legend it works as a messenger. It crosses the short sea to the island of Príncipe in a single morning. Its feathers light up the air. It carries news of love, of a death in the family, and of a ship sailing. A boat leaves and the family waits. Seven colours, seven messages. The islands are small. Yet without this old post, the two islands would be silent to each other.",
    "The girl Nuna knew the bird's name the way you know a cousin's. Her father had left by boat, and she was waiting for a sign, not a gift of feathers. At midday the parrot landed in the mango tree. Her grandfather read the old message: her father was already on land, the journey had gone well. Nuna did not shout or reach out her hand. She gave it fruit and said: ‘Take him my hug when you go back.’ The bird ate and flew off.",
    "A neighbour, seeing the colours, wanted a feather for luck in his pocket. He climbed the tree with a cloth and almost grabbed the tail. The parrot escaped and, that day, carried no message at all. Nuna's grandmother was firm: ‘The real bird is under threat. The legend asks us not to take its feather.’ The luck you tear from a winged cousin is not luck. It is a postbox shut.",
    "Nuna went to the tree and left fruit on the ground. She apologised in a quiet voice, even though it had not been her who reached out with the cloth. The next day the parrot came back, whole, with all seven colours in place. It brought the hour the boat would return. The family went to the quay in time. Between the two islands, the messenger crossed the short sea again, like someone delivering a letter. It asked for no ink and no paper.",
    "The São Toméan child learns this way that folklore and care for the bird are the same warning. Without the parrot, São Tomé and Príncipe would lose the old post that linked its homes. You do not keep a colour in your pocket. You keep the path open in the morning, from island to island. If you see the seven colours in the woods, leave the feather where it is. The message still has to reach someone.",
  ],
  "st:2": [
    "At Praia das Conchas, in São Tomé, the sand is pale and the ground crunches with small shells. In the shallows a mermaid sings, and the warm sea of the gulf seems made for that voice. The fishermen leave the first sardine in the water before keeping the rest in the basket. Sometimes people say she is a mãe-d'água, a water mother, who came from Angola. Sometimes she is simply the island having an owner, with a home in the breaking waves.",
    "The boy Tito was collecting shells to fill a whole bucket. His grandmother, sitting in the shade, said the beach exists to be seen, not emptied. ‘Leave one behind,’ she asked. ‘And leave the first sardine, if you ever go to sea.’ Tito laughed, because the net was not his yet. Even so, he kept all the shells in his pocket. That afternoon the song of the shallows seemed further away. It was like someone closing a window.",
    "The islands look towards Africa and, on the other side of the wide sea, towards Brazil. The mermaid of this tale speaks to both shores, without choosing a single home. At night the fishermen heard the voice and found their nets too light. One of them remembered the old rule. He threw the first sardine into the shallows, with respect and with no celebration. The water accepted the small payment. The singing came back, low, above the pale sand. At home, Tito heard the report and looked at his full bucket.",
    "Tito went back to the beach in the morning and poured half the bucket where the shells had been. He kept a few, the ones that fitted in one hand. He left the rest to the island's owner. He did not ask for gold or a fairy-tale wish. He asked that the sea keep giving fish to those who treat the water as a neighbour. A wave covered the shells and drew back, glittering. That was enough of an answer. One shell on the sand already pays for respect.",
    "The legend asks for little. If the sea is paid in respect, it gives fish back and gives the song back. The child who collects shells at Praia das Conchas always leaves one, so the shore is not left empty. São Tomé keeps this owner in the warm shallows, between Africa and the wide sea. If you hear the singing, do not run to look at her hair. Leave the first sardine and go back to your net. The sea says thank you in silence.",
  ],
  "st:3": [
    "On the plantations of São Tomé, cocoa still smells in the old groves, between tall trees. In other times the island was among the biggest producers in the world, and the bean travelled far in sacks. For children there is a gentle legend. Cocoa sweetens whatever it touches, if it was not harvested in anger. The fruit is magical because it is plant, rain and a calm hand. Without that calm, the sweetness lies.",
    "The girl Lina helped her grandmother open the ripe fruits, taking care not to spoil the seed. A man from the plantation, tired and angry, tore the fruits off in a hurry. He banged the basket on the ground. At night they made the hot drink. The part harvested in anger tasted bitter, and the part harvested slowly sweetened the cup. The grandmother did not call it a spell. She called it attention. Lina tasted both and noticed the difference, with no more explanation needed.",
    "Then the grandmother told the part that does not get sweetened with make-believe. On the old plantations there was forced labour. There were people who were not free, and a chocolate that reached the world with a human price. That is not erased just to make history pretty. Lina listened in silence. The magic bean still smelled good, but she understood that real sweetness asks for free hands, not forced ones. The grandmother spoke slowly, so that the truth would fit without a fright.",
    "The people of São Tomé stayed on this land, even after the hardship. They made a home of the plantation and, in time, a country. They sang the tchiloli under the trees and gave names to the plots. They taught their children to know the fruit without hatred. The greatest miracle was not the bean shining. The people turned a place of tiredness into a place of life. Cocoa now asks for different treatment. Lina kept this idea together with the sweet smell on her hands.",
    "The child deserves the sweetness and the truth, both on the same plate. The magic is the plant, which sweetens when the harvest carries no anger. The greatest miracle is the people having stayed and made a country of the plantation. If one day you drink cocoa from São Tomé, remember the smell of the groves and also the old price. The bean asks for free hands. That way the sweetness does not lie. The plantation can smell of home, and not only of old tiredness.",
  ],
  "st:4": [
    "The tchiloli is popular theatre from São Tomé and Príncipe. It is performed outdoors under the trees, and it can last for hours. It came from a European story, the tragedy of King Charlemagne, and the island made it its own soul. It is not a myth of the creation of the world. It is a rite, and we say so plainly. In the yard there are masks, dance steps and figures that everyone already knows before the play begins.",
    "The king enters, then the traitor, then the messenger. The king stands for the distant emperor, now dressed in the folklore of the island. The traitor breaks trust with a well-spoken lie. The messenger crosses the yard and brings the heavy news, without shouting. The masks help you see who is who, even from far away. The children sit on the ground. Nobody needs a fight to understand that a false word can bring down a house.",
    "The story of a Frankish king came into the Forro language, so the people hear it as their own. The emperor stops being just a name in a book. He begins to dance in the yard, among neighbours. Paulo, a boy from the plantation, followed the messenger with his eyes. That was the role most like a cousin bringing messages. The play was in no hurry. The forest was not either. The masks barely shone, and even so the plantation knew every role.",
    "When the king falls to the ground in the story, the yard goes quiet for a moment. It is not a fall of fear. It is the weight of betrayal, shown so that people can learn from it. Then the music comes back, the masks greet one another, and the day goes on. Paulo asked whether the king had finished. His mother said that in the theatre of the island nothing ends all at once. The next year the same king gets up again. Paulo understood, and stayed to the end.",
    "São Tomé explains itself on this stage. A small island can swallow an old empire and give it back in song. It does so unhurried, under the trees. The children see the king fall and see him rise again the following year. The traitor teaches the price of a lie. The messenger teaches the duty to speak. The tchiloli teaches that a story from outside can become home, if the people dance it as their own. The next year the yard fills again under the trees.",
  ],
  "gq:0": [
    "In mainland Equatorial Guinea, the jungle of Monte Alén shelters gorillas and ancient trunks. The Fang speak of the evu, the force each person carries inside. They also speak of the lords of the forest, who are not seen at first. The spirit of the forest is older than the park. Anyone who enters only to hunt too much finds the path curling up. Anyone who asks the ancestor's permission sees the animal from far away. It comes as a blessing, not as a stage.",
    "The hunter Ndong went in with more baskets than his household hunger asked for. He wanted a gorilla to boast about, not to eat in measure. After an hour, the trail he knew was going round in circles. The same roots appeared again, and the sun did not help him choose a direction. Ndong sweated and grew angry. The forest did not bite him. It ate his sense of direction, which is the punishment in this story. Without direction, the body's strength is worth nothing.",
    "He sat on a root and remembered what his grandmother used to tell Fang children: the forest is not empty. He asked permission in a quiet voice. He said the name of the spirit before any name from a book. He promised to hunt only for fair hunger. The path unrolled a little. In the distance, between leaves, a gorilla passed slowly and was not chased. Ndong saw it as one sees a lord, from far away, and lowered the spare basket.",
    "He went back to the village with little, just enough for the pot. He also carried the useful shame of someone who almost got lost. He told them that the gorilla has an invisible lawyer in the forest. If the hunger is fair, the hunter gets through. If the wish is greed, Monte Alén curls the trail until the person understands. The park, with its new rules, came later. The legend was already acting as a guard when there were no signs or map at the entrance.",
    "The Fang child hears the spirit first and only then the scientific name of the animal. That way the forest does not become scenery. It becomes someone's home. From then on, Ndong left one basket at home before setting out. Looking from far away, when the ancestor allows it, is already enough of a feast. If you go into the forest of Monte Alén, ask permission. The short path is the path of measure. The curled path is the path of hunger that never ends.",
  ],
  "gq:1": [
    "In the Fang tales of Equatorial Guinea, the leopard is elegant and the turtle is slow. Even so, the law does not always run with the best-looking. One day the leopard laughed at her short steps. The turtle did not shout. She set a race to the big tree, at sunrise. At night, she called her relatives and placed one at every bend of the trail. They had orders to answer when the leopard called. The plan came from her head, not her paws.",
    "In the morning the leopard shot off, sure of winning. Halfway along he shouted ‘Where are you?’. A turtle further ahead answered ‘I'm already here’. He ran faster. At every bend the same sentence came from further away, and his elegant chest began to lose patience. He did not see that they were relatives, alike in shell, waiting at their posts. The real turtle went slowly, in no hurry to look fast. The finish line already had a voice.",
    "At the tree, a relative sat waiting, as if she had arrived long ago. The leopard arrived out of breath and without the composure he valued so much. ‘Again,’ he asked, angry. The turtle accepted a second game, now without a race. ‘Go into my hole,’ she said, ‘if you fit its size.’ The leopard, vain, put in his head and shoulders. The hole was the size of the turtle. He got stuck in his own vanity, without injury. He was simply unable to come out and pretend nothing had happened.",
    "He had to ask for help, in a voice quieter than his morning roar. The turtle called her relatives and they widened the earth enough for him to back out. There was no beating. There was shame. The leopard licked his wounded pride, which is what you lick when teeth do not win. He left without looking back. The turtle, ugly to him and calm to the forest, was home by the time the sun was high.",
    "Equatorial Guinea laughs along with the Fang, and the child imitates both. There is the leap of the leopard and the short step of the turtle. The moral does not need shouting. The beauty of the tooth does not win the race of the head. Anyone who sets the meeting and thinks about relatives gets there first, even slowly. Anyone who trusts only in shine loses patience and ends up stuck in a small hole. The turtle keeps the law. The leopard keeps the lesson.",
  ],
  "gq:2": [
    "Bioko, once called Fernando Pó, is the island where Malabo now stands. It did not rise by chance in the middle of the sea. A myth of Equatorial Guinea says that a friendly volcano spat out a home. The people would hide there from an old war on the coast. Another voice says that a god planted a tooth of fire, and from that tooth the island grew. The two accounts meet: there was fire, and the fire made shelter. Geology confirms the volcano. The legend confirms that the home was placed on purpose.",
    "The war squeezed the coastal villages. The families wanted no more shouting and no more endless fleeing. They looked at the sea. The volcano, like an enormous neighbour, raised smoke, then stone, then ground that the water could not swallow. It was not a punishment. It was a door. The people crossed in small boats and stepped onto warm earth, which cooled beneath their feet. Pico Basilé rose into the clouds, guarding the new place like a tall post.",
    "The sea around it became a moat. Anyone who came in a hurry to fight had to cross water before reaching the houses. The tooth-island offered peaks, shade, and a place to see the gulf. From there you could watch without being in the middle of the fear. The children of that time heard the old people say that the land had been planted, not found. Something found can be lost. Something placed asks for care, as a house someone built for you asks for care.",
    "Malabo, the capital, settled later on this island of spent fire. The girl Isabel, looking at Basilé in the clouds, used to ask her grandmother for the story of its birth. Her grandmother repeated the tooth, the friendly volcano and the moat of water. Isabel understood that the peak was not just a lovely view. It was the tall, quiet proof that the shelter had stayed. The old smoke no longer rose. Gratitude was what should keep rising, each time someone looked at the summit.",
    "The child of Bioko looks at the peak and hears the island's birth. It was not an accident on a map. It was placed, so there would be a home when the coast was not enough. There are shelters that fire builds, and those homes ask for gratitude, not just a view of the gulf. If you live in the shadow of Basilé, remember the friendly volcano and the tooth planted in the sea. The water around is still a moat. The island in the middle is still a shelter.",
  ],
  "gq:3": [
    "Among the Fang and Bubi villages of Equatorial Guinea, the talking drum carries messages. It carries them while feet are still on the way. It is not meaningless noise. The tones imitate speech, and so a careful person sometimes understands a word. The legend of the first drum begins with a tree that already spoke on its own at night. It had a voice of wood. A hunter called Okenve heard it and did not pick up his axe the way you pick up firewood. He stopped and asked.",
    "The tree said it could give its voice, if he did not treat it like any old trunk. Okenve asked the old people's permission and kept the agreement. He made a drum from the trunk and covered it with a well-stretched skin. The skin was prepared with respect, in no hurry for the hunt. Because the tree already knew names, the skin came to know them too. The first beat was not a celebration. It was a short message to the neighbouring village: ‘We're well, you can come.’ And the village understood.",
    "Since then the drum has run between the houses like a postman. One tone calls a person. Another tone announces that someone is arriving. Another asks for water or tells that the boat has left. Bubi and Fang children grow up listening and, now and then, catch a word without anyone translating. It is not confusing magic. It is language, made of beats. Okenve taught his son not to drum for a joke. A false name on the drum is a lie that travels fast.",
    "One afternoon his son beat a serious message: the grandmother asked the family to come before nightfall. The people came. Nobody got lost on the way, because the drum had already said the hour and the place. Okenve put his hand on the wood and thanked the tree that had agreed. Without that agreement, the trunk would be nothing but firewood for a fire. With it, it is a post service. A post service keeps names alive between two villages.",
    "Equatorial Guinea, a land of languages with tones, understands this story without effort. The drum does not imitate speech by chance. It imitates it because speech was already music before it was drum. The child who hears a name on the skin learns that tools ask permission. If the tree had not said yes, there would be no voice. If you send a message, send it right. The talking drum does not like to correct lies along the way.",
  ],
  "gq:4": [
    "On the mainland of Equatorial Guinea runs the Uoro river, on its way to the estuary. In that river lives a princess who is the mãe-d'água, the water mother, of that place. She wears a necklace of beads and a dress the colour of the current. She asks for no marriage and no trap-song. She asks for respect. The fishermen say her name before casting the net. To those who do not poison the water, she gives fish back. To those who pour poison in, she does not answer, and the river loses its voice.",
    "The fisherman Esono had a torn net and a tightening belly. Someone offered him a bottle, a well-known and ugly shortcut. It was fish poison, which spoils the water so the fish come without any work. Esono almost accepted. He threw in a drop, just to try, and the Uoro went quiet in an instant. The princess did not appear at the bend. Neither did the fish. The drop was small, but the law of the legend does not weigh poison in grams. It weighs the choice.",
    "Esono was ashamed. He pulled his net out and walked to the village with an empty basket. He told his daughter what he had done. She already knew the princess's name, because children learn that name along with the name of the river. ‘Dead water breeds no princesses,’ said the girl, repeating the saying of the old people. Esono threw the rest of the bottle away, far from the bank. He spent the day mending the net, stitch by stitch.",
    "The next morning he asked permission in a clear voice, with no bottle in his pocket. He fished with the clean net and took no more than the household could eat. The current had movement again. A glint of beads passed in the depths, like a short greeting, and the fish came into the mesh. It was not a market-stall miracle. It was the answer of someone who does not bargain with dead water. The Uoro flowed on to the estuary, alive, carrying the princess's name to the sea.",
    "Equatorial Guinea tells this as a law, older than any poster. The child sees the river and remembers the princess of beads. Folklore, here, is a campaign against poison. The princess gives fish back to those who ask permission and fish cleanly. She does not argue with dead water, and neither do the fish. If you go to the Uoro, leave the bottle at home. A river paid in respect still reaches the estuary with a voice.",
  ],
  "gh:1": [
    "Before the stories, the world was a quiet place. People ate, walked and slept, but had no tales to put between the day and the night. The sky, Nyame, kept the stories safe like someone guarding gold. The fire lit faces with no story in them. Anansi, the spider, grew tired of the silence. He did not want just one joke for himself. He wanted the world to be able to tell itself. He climbed up to negotiate. That negotiation is the myth of literature in miniature, and deserves its own page beside the other story.",
    "Nyame listened and did not laugh at the spider. He said that every story had a price, and that the price was paid in tests. The python would be one chapter. The leopard would be another. The wasps' nest, another. The fairy of the forest, another still. Anansi accepted. He did not carry a sword. He carried conversation, soft rope, a gourd and a sticky doll. Each creature he persuaded, without hurting it, was a page taken out of the chest. The silence below did not yet know it was nearly over at the first fire.",
    "When the python agreed to be measured, a chapter came loose. When the leopard fell for the trick without being hurt, another chapter came down. When the wasps went looking for shelter and the fairy stopped to play with the doll, the chest grew lighter. Anansi did not steal the stories. He paid for them, one by one, with work. Ghana teaches this before school: stories cost something. Someone had to go and fetch them. They did not fall from the sky like free rain. They came because one small creature refused to stay silent.",
    "On the first night, Anansi opened the treasure beside the houses. One person told a story. Another answered. A child repeated it and changed a detail, and that detail stayed too. Stories, as soon as they arrive, begin to walk. At last the world could tell itself its own stories. The sky did not grow poorer. It traded a closed treasure for a people who knew how to talk about what they lived. The fire, which before only kept people warm, became a library. Silence was left for those who choose it, not for those with no voice.",
    "That is why Anansi is forgiven for other tricks. He was greedy and brilliant, but he did the work that was missing. How stories came to the world is not an extra decoration beside the tale of the spider. It is that tale seen through the empty land that filled up. If you hear a story at night, you are already at the end of this legend. Someone made a bargain for you. Each chapter had a fair price, paid without cruelty. Ghana keeps the memory of that first ‘listen’. Since then, the world has never gone back to silence only.",
  ],
  "gh:2": [
    "In Ghana, the talking drum atumpan is more than rhythm. It says the names of chiefs and it says proverbs. The legend of the first drum begins with a hunter in the forest, late in the afternoon. He heard a tree speaking on its own, with tones rising and falling like a sentence. It was not the wind. It was a voice. The man stopped, asked permission, and asked whether the tree wanted to go on speaking when people were far away. The tree, says the story, thought for a moment and agreed with a long murmur.",
    "The hunter did not cut it down as he would cut ordinary firewood. He made a drum body with the tree's agreement. He covered it with antelope skin, as real drums still are today. The voice stayed in the skin. When hands struck it, the tones imitated Akan speech. A sentence could travel from village to village faster than a tired messenger. Without the tree's agreement, it would have been noise. With it, it was speech. The forest had lent its tongue, and people promised not to spend it in vain.",
    "In the Asante palace at Kumasi, drums announce who is arriving. They also remind people of what custom asks. A child at the door hears the king's name and, sometimes, catches a word. The language and the drum use the same ladder of tones. The legend and the way of speaking meet there, with no argument. The old people smile when a young one repeats the right proverb. A palace is not understood by its walls alone. It is understood by this pulse. Anyone who cannot hear the atumpan misses half the house and half the message.",
    "There was a day when a message of peace had to cross a long road. The drums spoke before dawn, short and clear, and the villages opened their doors without confusion. Nobody had to shout until hoarse. The old tree, even transformed, still knew names. The hunter of the legend was gone, but the agreement continued: beat to say, not beat to frighten. In this story, Ghana plays what other peoples write down. The skin keeps the sentence. The hands act as the pen.",
    "If you hear a drum and think only of a celebration, come closer. In Ghana it also speaks. The child of Kumasi grows up among proverbs that can be danced and repeated. The tree agreed, the skin remembered, and the palace still listens. Without that agreement, the trunk would be firewood and the afternoon would be silent. With it, the message crosses the air. Treat the drum as you treat a voice. It is not a toy for drowning out conversation. It is the conversation, when words decide to go from tone to tone, all the way to the next house.",
  ],
  "gh:3": [
    "In the Akan forest, the palm trees are high and the harmattan wind dries the air. The leopard liked to remind everyone that he had teeth. The turtle moved slowly under the leaves and never argued about volume. One day the leopard blocked her path. ‘Whoever has no tooth has no right to speak,’ he said. The turtle blinked. ‘Then let's race to the big palm tree at sunrise. Whoever gets there first picks the subject.’ The leopard laughed. The race seemed like an easy prize to win.",
    "At night, the turtle spoke to her family. She did not ask for wings. She asked for places. One cousin stayed at the start. Another halfway along the path. Another near the palm tree. All of them would answer, if they heard the name: ‘I'm already here.’ It was not strength. It was a map of voices. Anansi, up in a web, saw the plan and did not spoil it. He only remarked, quietly: ‘The elegant one will lose his composure. Elegant ones fear that most.’ The forest went quiet, waiting for the first sun.",
    "At dawn, the leopard ran like a hurried king. Soon he shouted: ‘Turtle, where are you going?’ The voice ahead answered: ‘I'm already here.’ He ran faster. Halfway, the same sentence. Near the palm tree, again. When he arrived, panting, a turtle was sitting in the shade. She was calm, as if she had waited since the night before. The leopard lost his swagger. He puffed, turned in circles and forgot his elegance. The turtle did not bite. She only said: ‘A tooth is not an argument. I used my head.’",
    "Anansi came down on a thread and laughed without malice. ‘I collect stories,’ he said. ‘This one stays.’ The leopard looked at his teeth and saw that they had not served as an argument. He went to the stream to wash his coat. He told nobody what had happened. Composure, once lost, is hard to pick up from the ground. The turtle went back to her family and shared the shade of the palm tree. In the harmattan, even pride dries quickly. The Akan forest has a stage for the clever. That day, the stage was a race thought through.",
    "The child in Ghana chooses their clever one. Sometimes it is Anansi, sometimes the turtle. The moral, a familiar one, does not change. A tooth does not win a conversation. The turtle, with no teeth, argued better and left the leopard washing his coat in silence. If you are the strongest in the game, do not block the path for others. If you are the slowest, you can still think before sunrise. The palm tree stays where it was. The dry wind passes. And the leopard, elegant again, pretends the race never happened.",
  ],
  "gh:4": [
    "There was a time when a people of Ghana were thirsty. The wells failed and the ground cracked. Then a great snake, wise and with no wish to do harm, lay down on the land. ‘Walk beside me,’ she said. ‘I will stay, so that you can drink.’ Her body filled with water and turned into a river. That is how the Volta was born, not as a punishment. It was a relative who agreed to lie down so that life could go on. The villages on the bank learned the name. They learned not to treat the water as a dull thing with no will of its own.",
    "The water mothers and the goddesses of the river stayed with the snake in that memory. Anyone who fishes asks permission. Anyone who travels by boat does not dirty the riverbed for fun. In some houses a white cloth is still left on the bank. It is simple, with no theatre. It is a thank you. The child sees the cloth and understands that the river has a will of its own. If the water sickens, the fish leave. The lady of the current does not bargain with a dead riverbed. The Volta answers better to those who treat it as family than to those who only use it up.",
    "The years changed the map. The Akosombo dam held back the water and made an enormous lake, and electricity came to the houses. The spirit, it is said, had to learn a new riverbed. The new riverbed is wider, more still in some places, and still a river in others. It did not disappear. It learned. Modern Ghana and ancient Ghana share this water without pretending the map is the old one. The snake of the legend stretched out again, now also as a lake. It went on giving drink, and also giving light to the nights.",
    "On the bank, the two rites look at each other without hurry. There are electricity poles and there are white cloths. Some people work at the dam. Some grandmothers speak quietly to the water before a journey. Nobody needs to wipe out one rite for the other to exist. The child sees the enormous lake and hears the snake. They learn that electricity came and that the offering, in some houses, did not stop. The relative does not get angry with the dam if respect continues. It does get angry when water is treated as rubbish. That is when the legend turns serious.",
    "The message of the Volta fits in a sentence both old and new: treat the river as family. The snake lay down so the thirst would end, and stayed. The lake of Akosombo is the recent chapter of the same body. If you see a light switched on in Ghana, there is ancient water in that path. Leave, in your thoughts, the white cloth. Ask permission. Do not spoil what ends thirst. The spirit has learned a new riverbed. People can learn too. The two Ghanas sit on the same bank and look at each other.",
  ],
  "et:0": [
    "Makeda was the queen of Sheba, on the Ethiopian highlands, and the people still say her name with respect. She heard of Solomon, king of a distant land. He was famous for wisdom and not only for gold. Makeda did not want empty presents. She wanted questions. She prepared camels, loaded gold and set off, with her heart full of doubts. Nobody at court could answer those doubts. The journey was long, and every day on the road was already a lesson. She told those travelling with her that a queen also learns, and that gold without a question stays mute.",
    "In Jerusalem, Solomon received her without hurry, and Makeda put her questions on the table like loaves of bread. She spoke of justice, of rain, and of how a king should listen to the poorest before the richest. Solomon answered calmly. Sometimes he kept quiet, so the word would not come out crooked. She did not kneel in fear. She bowed her head because wisdom deserved respect. The gold she brought seemed small beside the answers. The two of them talked for many days, in no hurry to say goodbye.",
    "Their time together was not only questions. Makeda returned to Ethiopia with a new idea of justice and, later, with a son, Menelik. The tale needs no more than this to be true. Menelik grew up hearing the name of his father and the name of his mother. His mother was the queen of questions. When he became a man, he travelled too, to meet Solomon and to learn the line of the family. He carried the respect of the highlands, not an army. He listened before asking for anything.",
    "The sacred legend, the Kebra Nagast, says that Menelik returned with the Ark of the Covenant. This is the holy chest that Solomon's people kept. He did not carry it off like a highway robber. Ethiopian faith says the Ark chose the road to Axum. It has stayed there on the mountain ever since, hidden. Makeda, queen again at home, saw her son arrive. She knew her journey had changed the country. The gold was spent. The story stayed on its feet.",
    "The dynasty that called itself the child of this line lasted in memory until the twentieth century. It is a national myth and a book of the Ethiopian church. It is told with gravity, not with laughter. The child of Axum hears the name of Makeda and learns of a queen of questions, not only of gold. The Ark, says the faith, is still in the city. Legend and church cannot be separated. If one day you ask a question with respect, you are already walking a little like her.",
  ],
  "et:1": [
    "On the Ethiopian highlands, the lion is not just an animal of the savannah. It is the Lion of Judah, an ancient symbol of Solomon and of the line of the queen of Sheba. In church paintings it appears beside Saint George, and on old coins the mane fills the metal. The legend says one simple sentence: the lion does not lie down while the people are standing. It is not a beast that eats the village. It is a guard who stays awake when the night falls cold over the teff fields.",
    "It is told that in a time of thirst, people looked at the throne and the fields at once. A shepherd saw a lion on top of a rock. The lion was still, not leaping on the flock. The animal roared once into the valley, and the sound seemed to say the land was still being cared for. Nobody threw a spear at it. The shepherd lowered his stick and waited. The lion went down into the shade and let the sheep pass. In this tale, strength chose to hurt nobody.",
    "Haile Selassie was a real emperor, a man of flesh and blood. It is honest to say that he entered the myth while still alive. Many saw him as the Lion of Judah, heir to that ancient line. The tale does not ask us to forget the true history of his years. It asks us to understand the symbol. An old country recognises itself in an animal that does not run away. In Addis Ababa, the lion's name moved from old flags into family conversations, with pride and with care.",
    "Across the sea, in Jamaica, the Rastafari movement looked towards Addis Ababa and towards this same lion. It is not the same home or the same language, but the symbol travelled. The drum there and the church here recognise the mane. Ethiopia, at the centre, is still the land of the story, not a borrowed drawing. When an Ethiopian child sees the lion painted on stone, they see the highlands with a face. The mane is the line of Sheba, long and golden.",
    "The story mixes throne and parable, and both fit if they are told with respect. The lion, in this tale, does not eat the village: it guards the line of Sheba. Without a mane, the highlands felt bare, say the old people. It was like a hill with no cloud for a hat. If you see the old flag or a church painting, remember the sentence. The lion does not lie down while the people are standing. Standing is everyone's work, not just the roar of one.",
  ],
};
export default PART;
